Saving for retirement is crucial because it allows individuals to maintain their standard of living after they stop working. Without retirement savings, individuals may have to rely solely on Social Security benefits, which may not be enough to cover all their expenses.
To illustrate the importance of retirement savings, let's compare two scenarios: one where an individual saves for retirement and another where they do not.
Assumptions:
- Retirement age: 65
- Life expectancy: 85
- Annual expenses: $50,000
- Inflation rate: 2%
- Investment return: 6%
Here's a comparison table of the two scenarios:
| Scenario | Retirement Savings | No Retirement Savings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Annual savings | $10,000 | $0 |
| Total savings at retirement age | $500,000 | $0 |
| Annual retirement expenses | $50,000 | $50,000 |
| Years of retirement expenses covered | 20 | 0 |
| Total retirement expenses covered | $1,000,000 | $0 |
| Inflation-adjusted value of retirement expenses | $672,750 | $0 |
| Total investment returns during retirement | $1,167,000 | $0 |
| Inflation-adjusted value of investment returns | $784,050 | $0 |
| Total retirement income | $1,839,750 | $0 |
As you can see, the individual who saved for retirement has a total retirement income of $1,839,750, while the individual who did not save for retirement has no retirement income. The individual who saved for retirement was able to cover their expenses for 20 years, while the individual who did not save for retirement had to rely solely on Social Security benefits, which may not be enough to cover all their expenses.
